## Verified sess78's fix on the rig

Deployed and re-ran the guard probe. Both required conditions met (`HELD` alone would have been a failure):

```
VERDICT: HELD for 12s — flags stayed RECOVERY_GUARD
P236-HB-FOREIGN-WRITE slot=25 — own-slot CAS MISCOMPARE
P236-SELF-FENCE — stopping heartbeat, forcing shutdown
P236-WITHDRAW-REFUSED slot=25 — would destroy it
```

Against 0.11.416 the guard died at t+0.8 s and the victim logged nothing. The withdraw path is confirmed too. `release_slot` is still unexercised — it needs an unmount with a guard present. No `P-HB-SLOW` under the new build.

## Two new defects found and fixed

**The self-fence named a cause that hadn't happened.** Four detectors fire a self-fence; all four printed `device reformatted under live mount`. Three were false — the callback carried no reason, so the XFS layer hardcoded one. MXFS's most severe operator message told an admin their shared LUN had been destroyed when the truth was ordinary cluster fencing. Plumbed `enum mxfs_self_fence_reason` through both typedefs. Rig now shows `P131-SELF-FENCE [SLOT_TAKEOVER]: ... the device is intact`. The two SCSI-PR paths never went through the disklock log at all, so they only ever emitted the false line.

**`P49-STALEBASE` printed garbage.** 16 format specifiers, 15 arguments — the trailing `missing %d` had no argument, so `vsnprintf` read an uninitialised `va_arg`. `mxfs_pal_log` is `__printf`-annotated, so the DLM layer was already covered; this was the only mismatch in the tree.

## One hypothesis refuted, one thread left open

Twice a grep for `P236-WITHDRAW-REFUSED` returned nothing, then a later grep found it carrying an *earlier* timestamp. I built `tests/kmsg_visibility_latency.sh` and measured it rather than assume: worst lag 15.4 ms, 0/10 never-visible. **Refuted** — a single dmesg snapshot is sound here. So the missed greps have another cause and it is still unexplained. That matters more than the defect it was attached to: a grep that false-negatives on evidence that exists turns real failures into PASSes.

Also worth flagging: `tools/` binaries were missing from the tree entirely, and `prep_cluster` died with `FS_PREP_FAIL: mkfs tool not found` until I rebuilt them.

